examples.question_text:
Which histogram could represent approximately normally distributed data? Give a reason. Both use the same scales.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=5mm,y=3mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\begin{scope}
\node at (4,11) {$H$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(8,0) node[right] {Score};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,10) node[above] {Frequency};
\foreach \a/\b in {1/2,2/4,3/7,4/9,5/7,6/4,7/2} {\draw (\a-0.5,0) rectangle (\a+0.5,\b);}
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[yshift=-48mm]
\node at (4,11) {$J$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(8,0) node[right] {Score};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,10) node[above] {Frequency};
\foreach \a/\b in {1/9,2/7,3/4,4/2,5/4,6/7,7/9} {\draw (\a-0.5,0) rectangle (\a+0.5,\b);}
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Both peaks lie at $60$. Curve $B$ has twice the standard deviation, so it is wider and has half the peak height of curve $A$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[->] (-6.3,0)--(6.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[thick] plot[domain=-6.2:6.2,samples=120,smooth,variable=\x] ({\x},{2*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[thick,dashed] plot[domain=-6.2:6.2,samples=120,smooth,variable=\x] ({\x},{exp(-\x*\x/8)});
\path (0,2.35) node {$A$};
\path (3,0.65) node {$B$};
\draw[dotted] (0,0)--(0,2);
\draw (0,0)--(0,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$60$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The curves have equal widths and peak heights. Their peaks lie at $24$ and $36$, so curve $D$ is shifted $12$ units to the right of curve $C$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[->] (-5.3,0)--(5.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[thick] plot[domain=-5:2,samples=100,smooth,variable=\x] ({\x},{2*exp(-(\x+1.5)*(\x+1.5)/2)});
\draw[thick,dashed] plot[domain=-2:5,samples=100,smooth,variable=\x] ({\x},{2*exp(-(\x-1.5)*(\x-1.5)/2)});
\path (-1.5,2.35) node {$C$};
\path (1.5,2.35) node {$D$};
\foreach \a/\b in {-2.5/20,-1.5/24,-0.5/28,0.5/32,1.5/36,2.5/40} {\draw (\a,0)--(\a,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\b$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
These normal curves use the same axes. Identify the curve with:
(a) the largest mean;
(b) the smallest mean;
(c) the largest standard deviation;
(d) the smallest standard deviation.
Give reasons.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[->] (-8.3,0)--(8.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[thick] plot[domain=-8:8,samples=160,smooth,variable=\x] ({\x},{exp(-\x*\x/8)});
\draw[thick,dashed] plot[domain=-0.5:6.5,samples=100,smooth,variable=\x] ({\x},{2*exp(-(\x-3)*(\x-3)/2)});
\draw[thick,dotted] plot[domain=-4.75:-1.25,samples=100,smooth,variable=\x] ({\x},{4*exp(-2*(\x+3)*(\x+3))});
\path (0,1.35) node {$K$};
\path (3,2.35) node {$L$};
\path (-3,4.35) node {$M$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
(a) The total area is $1$. Symmetry divides it equally at the mean.

$\begin{aligned} &\mathrel{\phantom{=}}P(X>48)\\ &=\frac{1}{2}\times1\\ &=0.5 \end{aligned}$

(b) Since $43$ and $53$ are equally distant from $48$, the area between $48$ and $53$ is also approximately $0.34$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\definecolor{regionfill}{HTML}{DCEBE0}
\fill[regionfill] (1,0)--plot[domain=1:3.6,samples=70,smooth,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.4*exp(-\x*\x/2)})--(3.6,0)--cycle;
\draw[thick] plot[domain=-3.6:3.6,samples=120,smooth,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.4*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[->] (-3.9,0)--(4.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
\foreach \a/\b in {-1/43,0/48,1/53} {\draw[dashed] (\a,0)--(\a,{2.4*exp(-\a*\a/2)});\draw (\a,0)--(\a,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\b$};}
\path (-0.5,0.7) node {$0.34$};
\path (0.5,0.7) node {$0.34$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned} &\mathrel{\phantom{=}}P(X>53)\\ &\approx0.5-0.34\\ &=0.16 \end{aligned}$
